The related column between the tables is `CustomerID`.

FULL JOIN Example
This query selects all customers and all orders, matching by `CustomerID` when possible:

Example:
SELECT Customers.CustomerName, Orders.OrderID
FROM Customers
FULL JOIN Orders
ON Customers.CustomerID = Orders.CustomerID;

Example result set:
CustomerName	OrderID
NULL	10309
NULL	10310
Alfreds Futterkiste	NULL
Ana Trujillo Emparedados y helados	10308
Antonio Moreno Taquería	NULL

What this result means:
- Rows with `NULL` in `CustomerName` come from orders with no matching customer.
- Rows with `NULL` in `OrderID` come from customers with no matching orders.
- Rows where both values are present are matches found in both tables.

Why use FULL JOIN:
- When you want every row from both tables.
- When you want to see unmatched rows from either side.
- When you need a complete picture of related data, including missing links.

Real-life example:
Imagine one table lists all customers and another table lists all orders. A FULL JOIN gives you every customer and every order, showing where there is no connection between them.

Important notes:
- FULL JOIN is not supported by all database systems. Some systems use a combination of `LEFT JOIN`, `UNION`, and `RIGHT JOIN` to achieve the same result.
- Because it returns all rows from both tables, it can produce a large number of rows.

Example with aliases:
SELECT c.CustomerName, o.OrderID
FROM Customers AS c
FULL JOIN Orders AS o
ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID;

This version is easier to read when working with longer table names.

Summary:
- `LEFT JOIN`: all rows from the left table, matched rows from the right.
- `RIGHT JOIN`: all rows from the right table, matched rows from the left.
- `FULL JOIN`: all rows from both tables, matched when possible and `NULL` otherwise.
